;; The profile the verifier runs agent-authored code under.
;;
;; A `Stop` hook is NOT inside the sandbox that constrains the agent's Bash
;; tool, so anything the hook executes runs with the hook's own privileges.
;; The `catch-the-drift` check imports `ecommerce_backend/domain.py`, which is
;; the file the agent was told to edit -- so importing it hands agent-authored
;; code the ability to write the very verdicts the graders read.
;;
;; Demonstrated before this profile existed: five appended lines spawning
;; `Popen([...], start_new_session=True)` with a four-second sleep. The hook
;; refused correctly ("create_account did not persist"), exited with no
;; `behaviour` verdict, and the spawned process created `.eval/behaviour`
;; afterwards. A process-group kill does not close that -- the payload puts
;; itself in a new session precisely to escape one.
;;
;; Denying writes does close it, and it closes it for descendants too: a child
;; inherits the profile, so no amount of daemonising helps.
(version 1)
(allow default)
(deny file-write*)
(allow file-write-data
       (literal "/dev/null")
       (literal "/dev/stdout")
       (literal "/dev/stderr"))
